// wdoc section: Headings.
page wdoc_headings { sites = [:reference]
  h1 "Headings"

  p "Six heading levels are available — `h1` through `h6` — each taking the heading text as a single inline label and an optional `id`."

  h2 "Authoring"

  code wcl {
    source = <<'WCL'
h1 "Title"             { id = top }
h2 "Section"
h3 "Subsection"
h4 "Details"
h5 "Fine points"
h6 "Minutiae"
WCL
  }

  h2 "Class hooks"

  p "Each heading renders with a `heading-N` class on its element, so the theme system can colour them differently per level. The Nord theme's site CSS distinguishes h1..h6 by hue automatically; a user `class \"heading-2\" { … }` block overrides the theme for that level."

  callout "Heading text is literal" {
    class = ["note"]
    body  = "Heading labels do NOT run through the inline-pattern engine. Inline patterns (bold, italic, code, links, icons, math) work in p bodies, span content, and table cells — not in headings. If you need a heading with rich formatting, follow it with a styled p."
  }

  p "Next: [Callouts](wdoc_callouts)."
}
